#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544983} :activity/id #uuid "281bfa6d-174e-50b3-ac57-05c41fca1504" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f15c2a-e5fc-5ca0-bb85-07dbfb14f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.487" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544422} :answer-field/id #uuid "d936cedc-6149-5dd3-984d-e6f7a43f2a04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1e2b523-bf44-5c32-9b95-836b1e8d2e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-254609" :question/problem "While riding on a road, a cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $4$ potholes per hour. If the cyclist has not encountered any potholes in the past $30$ minutes, what is the probability that he will encounter a pothole in the next $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since the cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next pothole as an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, until the cyclist encounters the next pothole. Since the cyclist encounters, on average, $4$ potholes per hour (or $1$ pothole every $15$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ potholes per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 30+10\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.487.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cfd11fc-dd9a-5b3c-8a96-28e4560b2041"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa8381d-0308-5944-b790-50b787cd73b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544516} :answer-field/id #uuid "63853831-cd8f-5815-844b-593361f11ad4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d44e0172-2699-5b95-8b6b-c9e2c6a8cdf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-254581" :question/problem "A customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes. If a ticket has been open for $20$ minutes already, what is the probability that it will remain unresolved for at least another $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{5}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33983c3f-9e33-5e47-b127-51f52627feeb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596675b4-21d2-5ac4-b0a2-4839e83555d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccd3213-2b5a-598b-9a24-9bb6f2190670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8785" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544289} {:answer/id #uuid "d687dca3-0bd3-526a-a4ab-509c47139640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544547} {:answer/id #uuid "daa90ef2-43bc-5481-8ed8-ea54977434e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8892" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544558} {:answer/id #uuid "e7be2a9b-7b4a-533e-9a2f-8958ec2a1213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dfd42d-e350-5855-be90-2f0889abb592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddfe563-295a-5298-9f24-7cdb158be9e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132486" :question/problem "A monitor issues a warning signal when an action is needed as part of a production process. Signals occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $0.2$ minutes.  Determine the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the monitor issues a warning signal continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next signal appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between successive signals. Since the monitor issues a warning signal every $0.2=\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =5$ signals per minute.\n\nThe times between two successive signals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-5x}|}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "979ed7bb-94cd-5512-96ff-35ef9c614946" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5709fe-0354-5d44-b05a-02a21b0da97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} {:answer/id #uuid "d25214fe-4395-53a2-b548-fff04525e163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544534} {:answer/id #uuid "dbff2e28-f33a-5f18-8f52-e274e4c55978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6665" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544561} {:answer/id #uuid "e8872e6f-ba5a-570a-bbd3-ab8d4b6496ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544588} {:answer/id #uuid "fba8fc04-0fa8-577c-bd40-5fbcbbc648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c361c4-eefd-5311-9a84-4a9a4e2f34ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bc8f4760-f931-5737-8a9c-5aab0b30c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-132558" :question/problem "The waiting time for one customer to be served at a cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one person served every $4$  minutes. Determine the probability that the next person is served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbb06f0a-ea01-532e-b482-4baad0e7d648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45bde8d9-4c6e-5ee0-940a-5de511005e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} {:answer/id #uuid "70f23a81-9ddb-5bb1-b52a-a90210c0f213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8677" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544338} {:answer/id #uuid "75826e41-5f0a-5951-9d1b-962bf48353ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8596" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544348} {:answer/id #uuid "896a0afd-8d66-500b-9100-28316a76192d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8467" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544383} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc1e1f6-fe38-5508-ad8f-639f19a1b5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf80934d-3233-573f-8300-6e5e2ddf5899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1455aec3-db48-5efe-b5bf-59b8688bf438" :question/math-academy-id "q-132483" :question/problem "On a webpage, visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $15$ seconds.  Determine the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than $0.5$ minutes.  Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next visitor appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between two consecutive visitor arrivals. Since visitors arrive on average every $15$ seconds, the rate is $\\lambda =60÷15=4$ visitors per minute.\n\nThe time between two consecutive visitor arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than \n$0.5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e2ceb160-b573-55f7-b63a-adf4cc1b5784" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544954}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544072}]}
